## 2.3 Characterization
High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) and high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) (HAADF) images of the as-prepared products were acquired on a FEI Tecnai \(G^{2}\) F20 S-TWIN electron microscope at \(200\;\mathrm{kV}.\) The phase structure of the as-prepared product was characterized via X-ray powder diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8 advance with \(\mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{K}\alpha,\) \(\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~}\) ). The average Co NP crystallite size was estimated via the Scherrer formula
\[
D_{\mathrm{h}}=\lambda/(\beta_{\mathrm{h}}\cdot\cos\theta_{\mathrm{h}})
\]
where \(D_{\mathrm{h}}\) is the domain size of the diffraction line, \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the \(\mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{K}\alpha\) source used, \(\beta_{\mathrm{h}}\) is the width in radians of the diffraction peak measured at half-maximum intensity (fwhm, full width at half maximum) and corrected for instrumental broadening, and \(\theta_{\mathrm{h}}\) is the angle of the particular \((h k l)\) reflection. Raman spectra were recorded on a Renishaw RM-1000 with excitation from the \(514\,\mathrm{nm}\) line of an Ar-ion laser with a power of about \(5\;\mathrm{mW}.\) X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were recorded on a PHI quantera SXM
spectrometer with an Al \( \mathrm{K}\alpha = 280.00\,\mathrm{eV} \) excitation source, and binding energies were calibrated by referencing the C1s peak \( (284.8\,\mathrm{eV}) \) to reduce the sample charge effect. Magnetic hysteresis \( (M-H) \) curves were measured on a Physical Property Measurement System (PPMS–9T) at \( 300\,\mathrm{K} \) with an applied magnetic field \( (H_{\mathrm{max}} = 40\,\mathrm{kOe}) \). \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) sorption isotherms were measured on an ASAP 2420 Surface Area and Porosity Analyzer at \( 77\,\mathrm{K} \). Prior to measurement, the samples were degassed under high vacuum for \( 4\,\mathrm{h} \) at \( 523\,\mathrm{K} \). The specific surface areas \( (S_{\mathrm{BET}}) \) were obtained by applying the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) model to the portions of the adsorption branches of isotherms where \( P/P_{0} \) was between 0.05 and 0.35. The pore size distributions were evaluated using the Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) model.
## 2.4 Catalytic hydrogen generation
The catalyst \( (20\,\mathrm{mg}) \) and \( \mathrm{NaBH}_{4} \) \( (80\,\mathrm{mg}) \) were placed in a round-bottomed glass flask. Then, \( \mathrm{NaOH} \) \( (1\,\mathrm{g}) \) was dissolved in \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) \( (20\,\mathrm{mL}) \). The sodium hydroxide solution was added to the flask through a dropping funnel. The flask was immersed in a temperature-controlled water bath, which was placed on a magnetic stirrer with stirring rate of \( 500\,\mathrm{rpm} \). Hydrogen generation was studied via the traditional water displacement method [36]. Specific hydrogen generation rate \( (r_{\mathrm{B}}) \) was calculated using information from the stabilizing stages \( (140\,\mathrm{mL} \) of hydrogen generated) according the following formula
\[
r_{\mathrm{B}}=\frac{140(\mathrm{mL})}{t_{140}(\mathrm{min})\omega_{\mathrm{c}}(\mathrm{g})}
\]
where \( t_{140} \) is the time required to generate \( 140\,\mathrm{mL} \) of hydrogen, and \( w_{\mathrm{c}} \) represents the mass of \( \mathrm{Co} \) in the catalyst.
## 2.5 Recyclability tests
After the first cycle of hydrogen generation was completed, the catalysts were attracted to the bottom of the flask using a magnet, and the supernatant solution was removed. The separated catalysts were washed using water several times, dried and activated in air at \( 200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 22\,\mathrm{h} \). The catalytic hydrogen generation process was repeated 5 times.
## 3 Results and discussion
The synthesis strategy used to produce 3D core–shell \( \mathrm{Co-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C} \) is depicted in Scheme 1. The composite products are formed via precipitation and hydrothermal reaction, followed by annealing and thermal activation. During precipitation, Co-MOF nanocrystals are fabricated from cobalt nitrite and 2-methylimidazole in the presence of triethylamine. In the subsequent hydrothermal process, glucose carbonization is performed to generate a glucose polymer layer around the Co-MOF nanocrystals, resulting in Co-MOF@GP core–shell composites. After pyrolysis via annealing in a furnace with flowing nitrogen gas, \( \mathrm{Co@C} \) is produced via thermally induced decomposition of Co-MOF nanocrystals to form Co NPs and carbon shells, and simultaneous carbonization of the glucose polymer. Furthermore, activation encourages cobalt NPs to partially transform in to \( \mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) layers. Thus, \( \mathrm{Co-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C} \) 3D core–shell composites are generated.
The morphologies and structural evolutions of the composite materials were investigated via transmission electron microscope (TEM) imaging. The resulting Co-MOF@GP contains a core–shell structure with spherical nanocrystals as the cores and amorphous carbon as the shells (Fig. 1(a)). The core diameters
are in the range of \(30{-}90\;\mathrm{nm}\), and the carbon shell thicknesses are in the range of \(4{-}8\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1(a)). After pyrolysis at various temperatures, the resulting \(\mathrm{Co}@C{-}n\) is composed of several hollow carbon spheres, which are interconnected via graphitic carbon layers (Fig. 1(b), and Figs. S1(a) and S1(g) in the ESM). Each hollow carbon sphere contains several well-dispersed Co NPs encapsulated in carbon matrices. These Co NPs are derived from the pyrolysis of Co-MOF nanocrystals. The diameters of the Co NPs in \(\mathrm{Co}@C{-}\mathrm{II}\) range from 4 to \(14\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1(b)). The configuration of \(\mathrm{Co}@C{-}n\) is confirmed via HRTEM imaging (Figs. 1(c) and 1(d), and Figs. S1(b)–1(c) and S1(h)–1(i) in the ESM). The Co cores and carbon shells inside the hollow carbon spheres are observed clearly. \(\mathrm{Co-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}{-}n\) exhibit similar morphology to that of \(\mathrm{Co}@C{-}n\) (Fig. 1(e) and
Figs. S1(d) and S1(j) in the ESM). After activation, cobalt NPs partially transform into \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},\) as verified via XRD pattern analysis. The configuration of \(\mathrm{Co-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}{-}n\) is determined using detailed HRTEM images (Figs. 1(f)–1(h) and Figs. S1(e), S1(f), S1(k), S1(l), and S1(h) in the ESM). Energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) of \(\mathrm{Co-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{II}\) identifies the uniform distributions of C, Co, and O, which demonstrate oxidation of Co NPs during activation (Figs. 1(i)–1(l)).
Various diffraction features are observed in the XRD patterns of Co-MOF, Co-MOF@GP, Co@C-II, and \(\mathrm{Co-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{II}\) (Fig. 2(a)). As expected, the diffraction peak positions in \(\mathrm{Co-MOF@GP}\) correspond to XRD patterns simulated from single crystal structures of ZIF-67, confirming the formation and presence of
Co-MOF in the self-assembled hybrid [35, 37]. The XRD pattern of Co-MOF@GP does not exhibit any other peaks, indicating that the crystalline order of the Co-MOF host matrix remains unchanged after coating with GP. This is due to the formation of amorphous carbon during hydrothermal treatment of Co-MOF and glucose. The diffraction peaks of Co@C-II reveal the presence of Co and graphitic C. The broadened peaks at around \(25^{\circ}\) (2θ) are attributed to graphitic C (002) lattice facets. The peaks at \(44.2^{\circ}\) and \(51.5^{\circ}\) are ascribed to the (111) and (200) lattice facets of cubic \(\beta\)-Co (JCPDS card no. 89-4307) [34, 38]. The XRD pattern of Co-Co3O4@C-II contains additional peaks at \(31.2^{\circ}\), \(36.8^{\circ}\), \(59.3^{\circ}\), and \(65.2^{\circ}\). These additional peaks correspond to the (220), (311), (511), and (440) lattice facets of the pure cubic spinel phase of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) (JCPDS card no. 42-1467), respectively [39]. This result indicates the coexistence of Co and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs in Co-Co3O4@C-II. Co NPs are partially oxidized and
converted into \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\). Co@C-I and Co@C-III exhibit patterns similar to those of Co@C-II, meanwhile Co-Co3O4@C-I and Co-Co3O4@C-III exhibit patterns similar to those of Co-Co3O4@C-II, except for the XRD peak intensities (Figs. S2(a) and S2(b) in the ESM). The diffraction peaks enlarge and sharpen when samples are calcined at elevated temperatures. More detailed structural information about the material was provided via Raman spectroscopy analysis (Fig. 2(b) and Fig. S3 in the ESM). The D bands at about \(1,349\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and G bands at about \(1,588\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are clearly observed in the spectra of Co@C-n and Co-Co3O4@C-n. They demonstrate the presence of carbonaceous content, which can be attributed to the pyrolysis of Co-MOF and carbonization of glucose [40]. The calculated \(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}\) intensity ratios of Co@C-n and Co-Co3O4@C-n are 1.2 and 1.1, respectively, and are typical of disordered graphitic carbons. The 2D bands around \(2,690\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) suggest that there are several carbon layers in Co@C-III,
24.1 emu· \(\mathrm{g_{Co}}^{-1}\) ; 320 Oe, 5.1 emu· \(\mathrm{g_{Co}}^{-1}\) ; and \(430\;\mathrm{Oe}\) and 14.4 emu· \(\mathrm{g_{Co}}^{-1}\) , respectively (Figs. 4(b) and 4(c)). These characteristics, which are typical of ferromagnetism, aid in the effective magnetic separation of catalysts from the reaction system and magnetic momentum
transfer in batch or continuous flow reactors (Fig. S7 in the ESM) [31–33, 56, 57].
The catalytic activities of as-prepared samples during hydrolysis of \( \mathrm{NaBH_{4}} \) solution were investigated using a batch reactor. As in our previous reports,
the hydrogen generation reaction was conducted at 500 RPM in self-stirring mode [31–33]. A simplified diagram of the batch reactor and stirring mechanism are displayed in Fig. S8 in the ESM. First, the hydrogen generation reaction takes place with various samples used as catalysts at \(298\,\mathrm{K}\) (Fig. 5(a)). The \(r_{\mathrm{B}}\) of \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{I}\) and \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-I}\), \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{II}\) and \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-II}\), \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{III}\) and \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-III}\) are 455 and 2,100, 1,370 and 5,360, 1,950 and \(2,990\,\mathrm{mL\cdot min^{-1}\cdot g_{C o}^{-1}}\), respectively. The \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-n}\) sample exhibits a higher specific \(r_{\mathrm{B}}\) than the corresponding \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}-n\) and \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}@C}\) samples
(Fig. 5(b)). The excellent catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-n}\) may be attributed to the coexistence of \(\mathrm{Co}\) and \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}\) NPs inside the carbon shells. To explore the catalytic properties of the samples, \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{II}\) was treated in air at various temperatures and for various durations. Variations on \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C}\) are produced when \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{II}\) is treated at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(22\,\mathrm{h}\), or at \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(11\,\mathrm{h}\) or \(44\,\mathrm{h}\) (Figs. S9(a)–9(c) in the ESM). In contrast, porous \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}-I}\) is obtained when \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{II}\) is treated at \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(22\,\mathrm{h}\) (Fig. S9(d) in the ESM). The XRD patterns suggest that partial oxidation can be performed
successfully in all tested conditions except treatment at \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(22\,\mathrm{h}\) (Fig. S10(a) in the ESM). The best hydrogen production is achieved when the samples are prepared at \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(22\,\mathrm{h}\) (\(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-II}\)) (Fig. S10(b) in the ESM). In addition, treatment of \(\mathrm{Co@C-II}\) and \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-II}\) in air at \(550\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) produces \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}-II}\) and \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}-III}\) (Fig. S11 in the ESM). \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}-II@GO}\) and \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}-III@GO}\) were also prepared to test the properties of \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}@C}\) (Fig. S12 in the ESM, for the preparation procedure). The XRD patterns confirm the formation of \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}\) and \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}@GO}\) (Fig. S13(a) in the ESM). \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}@C}\) exhibits better catalytic activity than pure \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}\) (Fig. S13(b) in the ESM). Regardless, the catalytic activities of \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C}\) are much higher than those of \(\mathrm{Co@C}\) and \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}@C}\) (Figs. S13(c) and S13(d) in the ESM). The excellent catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C}\) may be ascribed to synergistic effects from \(\mathrm{Co}\) and \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}\) NPs.
The kinetics of hydrolysis is dependent on catalytic activity and other factors. Varying the initial \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) concentration causes different effects on the performances of different catalysts. The \(r_{\mathrm{B}}\) of \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-II}\) decreases when the \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) concentration is \(0.05\,\mathrm{M}\), but is not influenced by higher concentrations (0.1 and \(0.2\,\mathrm{M}\)) (Fig. 5(c)). This may be because mass transfer is a limiting factor at low \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) concentrations. The catalysts significantly enhance the reaction kinetics when high-concentration substrates are used [58]. The \(r_{\mathrm{B}}\) of \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-II}\) clearly increases with the temperature because of faster movement by \(\mathrm{BH_{4}^{-}}\)ions and water molecules (Fig. 5(d)). As expected, the Arrhenius plot of \(\ln k\) vs. the reciprocal of the absolute temperature \((1/T)\) is a straight line (Fig. 5(e)). The Arrhenius energy \((E_{\mathrm{a}})\) of the catalytic reaction can be calculated using the following Arrhenius equation
\[
k_{0}=A\exp\left(-\frac{E_{\mathrm{a}}}{RT}\right)
\]
where \(k_{0}\) denotes the rate constant, \(A\) is the preexponential factor, \(R\) represents the ideal gas constant, and \(T\) is the reaction temperature. The slope of the Arrhenius plot provides the activation energy (\(E_{\mathrm{a}} = 37.12\,\mathrm{kJ\cdot mol^{-1}}\)). This value is lower than those reported with other Co-based catalysts [59–61]. The relatively low Arrhenius energy may originate from synergistic interactions between \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}\) and Co NPs. For a clear
comparison, the maximum \(r_{\mathrm{B}}\) and \(E_{\mathrm{a}}\) of other metal catalysts are listed in Table S3 (in the ESM).
The stability of \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-n}\) after recycling was evaluated via hydrolysis of \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\). The XRD pattern reveals that \(\mathrm{Co}\) and \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}\) coexist in the reused catalysts (Fig. S14 in the ESM). The catalyst \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-II}\) exhibits superior stability, nearly maintaining its initial activity after being used up to six times (97.4% of \(r_{\mathrm{B}}\) in first run) (Figs. 5(f) and 5(g)). This recycling stability is better than those of highly active Co-based catalysts reported in Refs. [22–33]. The core–shell nanostructure of \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-II}\) is preserved well after being used six times for hydrolysis of \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) (Fig. S15 in the ESM). The efficient catalytic properties of \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-II}\) may be caused by synergic effects between \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}}\) NPs and carbon shells. The carbon shells coated onto \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}}\) NPs isolate the \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}}\) NPs from each other on carbon films and large surfaces (Figs. 1 and 3). The relatively high surface areas are also responsible for catalytic reactions. They provide more active sites with easy mass transfer. Precise identification of the active phase in Co-based catalytic hydrolysis of \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) is a very complex challenge due to in situ characterization difficulties [62]. Generally, a core–shell composite structure of cobalt-cobalt oxide-(poly) borates forms in the initial stage of a catalytic reaction [63, 64]. The amorphous state of the surface region and presence of boron in the surface species are requirements for high catalytic activity in Co-based catalysts. Based on above hypothesis, the \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}}\) heterojunctions provide a satisfactory surface precursor for in situ formation of possible catalytically active phases (\(2\mathrm{CoO\cdot B_{2}O_{4}\cdot 4H_{2}O}\) or \(\mathrm{Co@B_{\alpha}O_{\beta}(OH)_{\gamma}}\)) [64, 65]. Some control experiments were conducted by leaching \(\mathrm{Co@C-n}\) and \(\mathrm{Co-Co_{3}O_{4}@C-n}\) with HCl to verify the process of diffusion through the carbon shells during the catalytic reaction. The graphitic carbon layer is inert to leaching with HCl. Photographic images of the acid leaching process are provided (Fig. S16 in the ESM). A pink solution is obtained after acid leaching. The pink color is likely due to the presence of \(\mathrm{Co^{2+}}\) ions in solution. Protons and \(\mathrm{Co^{2+}}\) diffuse freely through the carbon layer, which indicates that there are some pores in the carbon layer shells. These pores allow diffusion of the substrate and product molecules during catalysis.
## 4 Conclusions
4 ConclusionsHollow carbon sphere-wrapped Co NPs@C core–shell composites were constructed via pyrolysis of glucose polymer-wrapped Co-MOF nanocrystals synthesized via an in situ hydrothermal treatment. Thermal treatment in air tuned the composition of Co NPs@C by converting some \(\mathrm{Co}^{0}\) to \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and forming heterojunctions. These \(\mathrm{Co-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) heterojunctions in the nanoparticle cores provided very high catalytic activities for hydrogen generation via hydrolysis of \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\). The carbon shells protected the \(\mathrm{Co-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) cores from leaching, aggregating, and growing. Superior catalytic performance, including high activity and excellent stability were obtained. This method of fabricating and pyrolyzing core–shell MOF composites in confined spaces for construction of hierarchical core–shells with heterojunctions may have broad applications in areas of electro- or chemical catalysis that involve energy storage and conversion.
